rsx metal backend: a full record array is submitted mid-frame instead of dropping the rest of the frame; the cap
(ENG_MAX_RECORDS) may be defined at build time.

The Metal backend records at most 8192 draws, clears and copies per submission and dropped every record past that.
A Resistance 3 firefight records ~7800 draws a frame besides its clears and copies; the log showed "[rsx
engine/metal] dropped 117-195 record(s) (cap 8192)" every few frames throughout the Haven fight, i.e. geometry
missing from those frames.

Past the cap the backend now does what it already did for a full staging arena: it submits what has been recorded,
waits for it, and goes on recording (eng_record_room -> eng_flush_recorded). Two things that path got wrong are
fixed with it, since the cap now reaches it every heavy frame:
- the bound vertex/fragment constants were staged in the arena the commit hands off; the next draw read whatever
  the new arena held at their old offsets. They are staged again in the new one.
- eng_draw staged vertices and indices in two reservations; a second one that filled the arena submitted it and
  left the vertices' offset pointing into the next. They now share one reservation.
The draw engine already reads the commit serial after draw_cached, so a cached draw that opens the next submission
tags its block with that one.

The submission waits for the GPU, a stall in mid-frame, so the port still defines 16384 from its own build, like
RSX_DRAW_ENGINE_TEXTURE_CACHE: with it the scripted Haven run reaches the cap in no frame ("records in one
submission" is logged the first 4 times it happens).
A host built with a cap of 4096 submits mid-frame throughout the same 240 s run: no record dropped, no command
buffer failure, frames that match the 16384 run, at 15-16 fps against 23-28 (the wait).

libs/video/tests/test_metal_vertex_cache.m fills the array with clears of one target, binds known constants and
records a cached draw: the full array is submitted and completes without a failure, the draw is the first record of
the next submission, and its constants are byte-equal in the new arena; a staged draw's indices follow its vertices
at the next aligned offset. test_metal_rect_clears and test_metal_independent_clears get the display-gamma stub
the other backend fixtures have: their clears can now reach the commit path. With the old drop the first three checks fail, without the re-staging the constants
check fails. The macOS workflow builds the test a second time with -DENG_MAX_RECORDS=16384
-DRSX_TEST_EXPECT_MAX_RECORDS=16384: the test asserts the cap it was built with and the submission happens at 16384.

Upstream: PR #719 (standalone on master: its own test, libs/video/tests/test_metal_record_cap.m, instead of the vertex cache test used here).
